Chapter: BoardMmioPure

  cites Boards chapter Stm32F4Board

 Probe: a board driver's register access cannot be laundered through a
 pure signature.

 This is the companion to cap-launder-pure-mmio, and it exists because
 that probe passed for months while the thing it protects was wide open.
 cap-launder-pure-mmio calls the intrinsics directly, so it proved only
 that the intrinsics carry Device.Mmio. It could not see that the nine
 board chapters -- the only code in the tree that actually drives a
 register -- sat on the type checker's quire-effect-exemption list and
 were never checked at all. A driver typed `Integer -> Integer` read and
 wrote RCC, and nothing complained.

 So this probe goes through the driver, not around it.
 stm32-gpio-enable-port declares [Device.Mmio]; blink wants to be pure.
 The effectful call cannot hide in a plain let (CDX2033) and the effect
 is not declared in the signature (CDX2031).

Section: Definitions

  blink : Integer -> Integer
  blink (port-bit) =
   let w = stm32-gpio-enable-port port-bit
   in w

  opening : Text = show (blink stm32-rcc-gpiod-en)

